\title{A Verified Base Layer for the Ordered Field of Transseries:\\
Monomial Signs, Arithmetic, Square Roots, and Infinitesimals}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 24, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Transseries are formal asymptotic expansions that generalize power series by
admitting, besides powers of the variable $x$, powers of $\log x$ and of the
iterated exponentials $e^{x}, e^{e^{x}}, \dots$. They form a non-Archimedean
ordered field, the natural setting for asymptotic expansions ``beyond power
series,'' including the exponentially small phenomena invisible to classical
perturbation theory. We present a mathematically correct, machine-verified
\emph{base layer} for this field, realized as the lexicographically ordered Hahn
series field $\TSeries = \Lex(\HahnSeries\;\TransMono\;\RR)$ over the
transmonomial value group $\TransMono = \Lex(\ZZ \to_0 \RR)$. Working with the
single-term generator $\term(g,a)$ --- the series with sole coefficient $a$ on
the transmonomial $g$ --- we establish: (i) a sign characterization,
$0 < \term(g,a) \iff 0 < a$, with its negative counterpart; (ii) the monomial
multiplication law $\term(g,a)\,\term(h,b)=\term(g+h,ab)$; (iii) a corrected
square-root law for square-compatible monomials, tracking both exponent and
coefficient; (iv) the impossibility of a negative-coefficient monomial being a
square; and (v) the existence of positive infinitesimals, with an explicit
witness. We are explicit about scope: this is \emph{not} a proof of real closure
nor of general square-root closure, but the verified foundation those results
require. We give full statements, faithful proof sketches, supporting algorithms,
and a roadmap toward real closure.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Asymptotics beyond power series}
When a quantity is studied as $x \to +\infty$, its behavior is governed not by a
single number but by a hierarchy of \emph{scales of growth}. A power series
$\sum_n a_n x^{-n}$ captures the polynomially graded part of this hierarchy, but
it is structurally incapable of representing two ubiquitous phenomena: \emph{(1)}
trans-polynomial growth such as $e^{x}, e^{e^{x}}, \dots$ that exceeds every power
of $x$; and \emph{(2)} exponentially small corrections such as $e^{-x}$ or
$e^{-x^2}$ that are smaller than every power $x^{-n}$ and yet nonzero --- the terms
responsible for Stokes phenomena, resurgence, and long-time (in)stability of
dynamical systems.

\emph{Transseries} supply a single algebraic structure containing all such scales
at once. A transseries is a well-ordered formal sum of real multiples of
\emph{transmonomials} --- products of real powers of $x$, $\log x$, $e^{x}$,
$e^{e^{x}}$, and so on. The totality is a real, non-Archimedean ordered field with
remarkable closure properties.

\subsection{Contribution and scope}
Deep closure theorems for transseries rest on a foundation of elementary but
error-prone facts about \emph{monomials}: how their signs are determined, how they
multiply, when they admit square roots, and how infinitesimals arise. We formalize
that foundation with machine-checked proofs. We emphasize, exactly as the formal
development does, what is and is not claimed. We do \textbf{not} prove real closure
of the transseries field, nor square-root closure in general. We \textbf{do}
provide a verified base layer: monomial signs (Theorems~\ref{thm:pos},
\ref{thm:neg}), monomial arithmetic (Theorem~\ref{thm:mul}), valid square roots of
positive square-compatible monomials and the non-squareness of negative monomials
(Theorems~\ref{thm:sqrt}, \ref{thm:notsq}), constants as monomials
(Lemmas~\ref{lem:nat}, \ref{lem:one}), and positive infinitesimals with an
explicit witness (Theorem~\ref{thm:inf}, Lemma~\ref{lem:posexp},
Corollary~\ref{cor:explicit}).

\subsection{On the formalization and its guarantees}
Every statement in Section~\ref{sec:results} is a theorem with a complete,
machine-checked proof; the sketches below faithfully summarize those arguments,
naming the exact lemmas invoked. Two commitments matter. First, the hypotheses are
exactly those used: the square-root law carries both $a\ge 0$ and $g=k+k$ because
the proof needs both. Second, negative results are stated as positive theorems:
``a negative monomial is not a square'' is the proved Theorem~\ref{thm:notsq}, not
an informal caveat. This discipline is what makes the base layer safe to build on.

\section{The model}

We realize transseries via Hahn series, the standard device for endowing formal
infinite sums over an ordered value group with a field structure.

\begin{definition}[Transmonomial value group]\label{def:transmono}
The \textbf{value group of transmonomials} is
$\TransMono := \Lex(\ZZ \to_0 \RR)$, the additive group of finitely supported
functions $\ZZ \to \RR$ with the \emph{lexicographic order} (\texttt{Finsupp.Lex}):
for $g \neq 0$, the sign of $g$ is the sign of $g(h^\ast)$ at the \emph{smallest}
index $h^\ast$ on which $g$ is supported. The integer index $h$ is the \emph{tower
height} (height $0$ is the scale of $x$, height $1$ of $e^{x}$, height $-1$ of
$\log x$), and $g(h)$ is the exponent at that height; addition is pointwise. By the
convention of Definition~\ref{def:tseries} the dominant monomial of a series sits
on the smallest group element, so a strictly positive group element corresponds to
an infinitesimal scale (cf.\ Theorem~\ref{thm:inf}).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Field of transseries]\label{def:tseries}
The \textbf{field of transseries} is the lexicographically ordered Hahn series
field $\TSeries := \Lex\big(\HahnSeries\;\TransMono\;\RR\big)$. A Hahn series over
$\TransMono$ with real coefficients is a function $\TransMono \to \RR$ with
well-ordered support; this guarantees products are defined and the structure is a
field. The $\Lex$ wrapper installs the order in which a series' sign is the sign of
the coefficient on its smallest-index (dominant) monomial: writing $\lc(s)$ for
that \emph{leading coefficient}, $0 < s \iff 0 < \lc(s)$. Over $\RR$, this makes
$\TSeries$ a strict ordered field.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}[Why Hahn series, and why well-ordering matters]
The restriction that a Hahn series have \emph{well-ordered} support is exactly what
makes the formal infinite sum multiply and invert like a field element: in a
product each output monomial receives only finitely many contributions (so the
convolution coefficient is a finite sum); there is a well-defined dominant
(smallest) monomial, hence a leading coefficient and a sign; and recursive
inversion makes $\TSeries$ a field. The one-term series has single-point support,
so all of these mechanisms specialize to elementary, directly checkable
identities --- which is why the base layer can be made airtight before any
infinite-support reasoning.
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}[One-term series]\label{def:term}
For $g \in \TransMono$ and $a \in \RR$, the \textbf{one-term series} is
$\term(g,a) := \mathrm{toLex}(\mathrm{single}\;g\;a) \in \TSeries$, the series
whose only nonzero coefficient is $a$, on the monomial $g$. Its leading
coefficient is $a$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Explicit positive exponent]\label{def:posexp}
$\posExp := \mathrm{toLex}(\mathrm{single}\;(0:\ZZ)\;(1:\RR)) \in \TransMono$, the
transmonomial with unit real exponent at tower height $0$ --- the generator whose
one-term series $\term(\posExp,1)$ is the explicit infinitesimal of
Theorem~\ref{thm:inf} (it behaves like a reciprocal scale such as $1/x$ as
$x\to\infty$).
\end{definition}

\section{Main results}\label{sec:results}

Throughout, $g,h,k \in \TransMono$ and $a,b \in \RR$.

\subsection{Signs of monomials}

\begin{theorem}[Monomial positivity; \texttt{single\_pos\_iff\_coeff\_pos}]\label{thm:pos}
$0 < \term(g,a) \iff 0 < a$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The sign of a Hahn series equals the sign of its leading coefficient
($0 < s \iff 0 < \lc(s)$). For a one-term series the leading coefficient is
exactly the coefficient, $\lc(\term(g,a)) = a$ (after
$\mathrm{ofLex}\circ\mathrm{toLex}=\mathrm{id}$). Composing the two equivalences
gives the claim; the monomial $g$ is irrelevant to the sign.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Monomial negativity; \texttt{single\_neg\_of\_coeff\_neg}]\label{thm:neg}
$a < 0 \implies \term(g,a) < 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Dual to Theorem~\ref{thm:pos}, using $s < 0 \iff \lc(s) < 0$ and
$\lc(\term(g,a)) = a$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Arithmetic of monomials}

\begin{theorem}[Monomial law; \texttt{term\_mul\_term}]\label{thm:mul}
$\term(g,a)\cdot\term(h,b) = \term(g+h,\;a\,b)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
This is the $\Lex$-wrapped instance of
$\mathrm{single}\;g\;a \cdot \mathrm{single}\;h\;b = \mathrm{single}\;(g+h)\;(ab)$.
Convolution of two single-support series yields a single support point at $g+h$
with coefficient $ab$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Constants}

\begin{lemma}[Naturals as monomials; \texttt{natCast\_eq\_term}]\label{lem:nat}
For $n \in \NN$, $\;(n : \TSeries) = \term(0, (n:\RR))$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Unit as monomial; \texttt{one\_eq\_term}]\label{lem:one}
$(1 : \TSeries) = \term(0, 1)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch (both)]
The natural-number cast and the field unit are constant series supported on the
identity monomial $0$, with coefficient $n$ (resp.\ $1$) there. Equality of
coefficient functions transported across $\mathrm{toLex}$ gives the identities.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Square roots}

\begin{theorem}[Square root of a square-compatible monomial; \texttt{single\_square\_of\_double\_exponent}]\label{thm:sqrt}
If $g = k + k$ and $0 \le a$, then $\big(\term(k,\sqrt{a})\big)^{2} = \term(g,a)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand the square and apply the monomial law (Theorem~\ref{thm:mul}):
$\term(k,\sqrt a)^2 = \term(k+k,\;\sqrt a\cdot\sqrt a)$. Substitute $k+k=g$ and use
$\sqrt a\cdot\sqrt a = a$ for $a \ge 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The result tracks \emph{two} data: the exponent must be halvable ($g=k+k$) and the
coefficient nonnegative ($a\ge 0$). An informal ``square root halves the
exponent'' that ignores the coefficient is wrong; the formal statement corrects it
by replacing $a$ with $\sqrt a$ under $a\ge 0$. This is the precise algebraic seed
of the divisibility requirement on the value group (Section~\ref{sec:future}).
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Negative monomials are not squares; \texttt{not\_square\_negative\_monomial}]\label{thm:notsq}
$a < 0 \implies \neg\,\mathrm{IsSquare}(\term(g,a))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $\term(g,a) = r^2$, then $r^2 \ge 0$ in an ordered ring, so $\term(g,a) \ge 0$;
but Theorem~\ref{thm:neg} gives $\term(g,a) < 0$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Infinitesimals}

\begin{theorem}[Positive infinitesimal monomial; \texttt{positive\_infinitesimal\_monomial}]\label{thm:inf}
For any $\delta \in \TransMono$ with $0 < \delta$, the monomial
$\varepsilon := \term(\delta,1)$ satisfies $0 < \varepsilon$ and
$(n:\TSeries)\cdot\varepsilon < 1$ for every $n \in \NN$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Positivity is Theorem~\ref{thm:pos} with $a=1$. For infinitesimality, rewrite via
Lemmas~\ref{lem:nat}--\ref{lem:one} and the monomial law:
$(n)\cdot\varepsilon = \term(0,n)\cdot\term(\delta,1) = \term(\delta,n)$, while
$1 = \term(0,1)$. Comparing these one-term series in the Hahn order reduces to the
dominant index $0$: since $0 \neq \delta$, the series $\term(\delta,n)$ has
coefficient $0$ at index $0$ whereas $1$ has coefficient $1$ there, and both
vanish below $\delta$. Hence $\term(\delta,n) < 1$ for all $n$. Intuitively, the
product lives on the growing magnitude $\delta$ and is dominated by the constant
$1$ on the bottom rung.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{posExp\_pos}]\label{lem:posexp}
$0 < \posExp$ in $\TransMono$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Compare $\posExp = \mathrm{single}\;0\;1$ with $0$ lexicographically: they agree
(both $0$) below index $0$, and at index $0$ the value $1 > 0$ decides positivity.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Explicit infinitesimal; \texttt{explicit\_positive\_infinitesimal}]\label{cor:explicit}
Instantiating Theorem~\ref{thm:inf} at $\delta = \posExp$ yields the explicit
positive infinitesimal $\term(\posExp,1)$, a named computable witness that
$\TSeries$ is non-Archimedean.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{A worked example}
Let $s=\term(g,9)$ with $g$ the exponent $4$ at height $0$, so $g=k+k$ with $k$ the
exponent $2$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:sqrt}, $\sqrt{s}=\term(k,3)$ (since $\sqrt 9=3$
and $k+k=g$), and squaring back via Theorem~\ref{thm:mul} gives
$\term(k,3)\cdot\term(k,3)=\term(k+k,9)=s$. The naive rule that halves only the
exponent returns $\term(k,9)$, whose square is $\term(g,81)\neq s$ --- a concrete
witness that the coefficient must also be rooted. Meanwhile $\term(g,-9)$ is, by
Theorem~\ref{thm:notsq}, not a square. Finally, with $\delta=\posExp$ (positive by
Lemma~\ref{lem:posexp}), $\varepsilon=\term(\delta,1)$ satisfies $0<\varepsilon$
and $n\varepsilon=\term(\delta,n)<1$ for every $n$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:inf}): adding
$\varepsilon$ to itself $10^{18}$ times still does not reach $1$.

\section{Algorithms}

The verified laws are equational and decidable on finite data, inducing exact
symbolic algorithms.

\subsection{Monomial multiplication}
Realizing Theorem~\ref{thm:mul}: represent a transmonomial as a finite map
height~$\mapsto$~exponent and a one-term series as a pair (monomial, coefficient);
multiplication adds the maps and multiplies the coefficients. Complexity
$O(|g|+|h|)$.

\subsection{Monomial square-root decision}
Realizing Theorems~\ref{thm:sqrt}--\ref{thm:notsq}: given $\term(g,a)$, if $a<0$
report ``not a square''; if $a\ge 0$ and every exponent in $g$ is halvable in the
chosen index group, return $\term(g/2,\sqrt a)$; otherwise report ``not
square-compatible in this value group.''

\section{Applications and numerical demonstration}

Because the value group is concrete, all four laws can be exercised numerically by
grounding transmonomials at a large $x$ and checking that the symbolic identities
match floating-point reality and that the order predictions hold. The accompanying
demo implements $\term$, the monomial law, the square-root law, the sign rule, and
the infinitesimal construction, and verifies on concrete inputs (e.g.\
$\sqrt{9x^4}=3x^2$, $(3e^x)(2e^{e^x})$, and $n\cdot\term(\posExp,1)<1$) that the
formal predictions hold, including the failure of the naive exponent-only
square-root rule.

Three concrete uses follow: (i) exact symbolic asymptotics, since the monomial law
is an exact identity on finite data; (ii) certified sign determination, reducing
the sign of a one-term expansion to the sign of one real number
(Theorems~\ref{thm:pos}--\ref{thm:neg}); and (iii) detection of non-squares and of
the halvability obstruction (Theorems~\ref{thm:notsq}, \ref{thm:sqrt}) that drives
the design choice toward a divisible value group. The infinitesimal of
Theorem~\ref{thm:inf} is itself notable: an explicit positive element below every
$1/n$, the hallmark separating transseries from the real line.

\section{Discussion}

The development is deliberately conservative: airtight versions of the statements
every higher transseries theorem relies upon, with the enabling hypotheses
exposed rather than hidden (the coefficient condition $a\ge 0$ \emph{and} the
exponent condition $g=k+k$). It does \emph{not} assert real closure or general
square-root closure; stating these non-claims explicitly prevents the common error
of conflating ``monomials behave well'' with ``the field is real-closed.''

\section{Future directions}\label{sec:future}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{From monomials to finite-support polynomials.} Extend the sign
characterization (Theorems~\ref{thm:pos}--\ref{thm:neg}) to finitely supported
series: the sign of a nonzero finite sum is that of its leading coefficient; the
remaining work computes the leading coefficient of an explicit finite sum of
$\term$s.
\item \textbf{The leading-term valuation.} Formalize the map sending each nonzero
transseries to its dominant transmonomial ($\mathrm{orderTop}$) and prove
multiplicativity, ultrametric inequalities, and order-compatibility. The monomial
law (Theorem~\ref{thm:mul}) is the computational core.
\item \textbf{Divisibility of the value group.} Square-root closure needs a
2-divisible value group; real closure needs full divisibility.
$\TransMono=\Lex(\ZZ\to_0\RR)$ is not 2-divisible (the integer index obstructs
halving). Replacing the index by $\Lex(\QQ\to_0\RR)$ or passing to the divisible
hull makes Theorem~\ref{thm:sqrt} apply to every positive monomial; the explicit
hypothesis $g=k+k$ makes this requirement precise.
\item \textbf{Square roots of arbitrary positive series.} Obtain $\sqrt s$ for any
positive $s$ by factoring out the (halvable) dominant monomial, reducing to
$\sqrt{1+u}$ with $u$ infinitesimal, and iterating leading-term cancellation;
convergence is controlled by the well-ordering of support. This is the gateway to
real closure.
\end{enumerate}

